  "response": "Distinguishing selection from drift begins with the recognition that the two processes leave their effects differently distributed across the genome, so that the first line of evidence is comparative rather than local. Genetic drift affects all loci across the genome in a similar manner, and natural selection acts on specific loci and produces localised outliers in genome-wide scans; a uniform response at every locus rules out localised outliers of that kind, so these two pictures of the genome cannot both be correct. There is also a quantitative threshold at issue. Genetic drift is strongest in populations with large effective sizes, and that cannot be squared with the standard condition under which drift overwhelms selection, namely that the selection coefficient be smaller than the reciprocal of twice the effective population size.\n\nThe theoretical scaffolding came first. The neutral theory of molecular evolution was proposed by Motoo Kimura, and only afterwards was the McDonald\u2013Kreitman test devised in order to put neutral expectations to the test. Tajima's D compares average pairwise nucleotide diversity with the number of segregating sites. The McDonald\u2013Kreitman test compares nonsynonymous and synonymous variation within a species to that between species. The McDonald\u2013Kreitman test is computed without any data from an outgroup species; that characterisation and a test whose very structure sets variation within a species against variation between species cannot both be true.\n\nA recent selective sweep reduces genetic diversity in the chromosomal region flanking the selected site. Demographic bottlenecks distort the site-frequency spectrum in ways that mimic the signature of a selective sweep. That mimicry follows directly from the construction of the summary statistics: because Tajima's D reads population history off the contrast between average pairwise nucleotide diversity and the count of segregating sites, a bottleneck that reshapes the spectrum is registered as though a sweep had occurred. Temporal data break the impasse. The trough of diversity flanking a selected site shows that the allele's frequency trajectory in the ancient DNA time series was inconsistent with neutral drift.\n\nThe allele's frequency trajectory in the ancient DNA time series was consistent with neutral drift; this reading and the departure from neutral expectation just established exhaust the alternatives, and exactly one of them can hold, never both. Whereas a trajectory that conforms to drift leaves the strength of selection unquantified, work on living populations attacks the problem directly. Reciprocal transplant experiments measure fitness differences between genotypes under field conditions; this evidence indicates that measuring lifetime reproductive success of different genotypes in the wild yields a direct estimate of the selection coefficient. Where genome-wide outlier analysis, demographically controlled frequency spectra, time-series data and measured fitness converge on the same conclusion, the question of whether the trait arose by selection or by drift can be regarded as settled.",
